.\"\"\"\n    all_tables = []\n    with pdfplumber.open(filepath) as pdf:\n        for page in pdf.pages:\n            tables = page.extract_tables()\n            all_tables.extend(tables)\n    return all_tables\n```\n\n## Important Notes\n- `extract_text()` returns `None` if the page has no extractable text.\n- For PDFs with simple lists (one item per line), splitting by newline works well.\n- pdfplumber handles multi-page PDFs automatically via `pdf.pages`.\n- Strip whitespace from extracted lines to handle formatting artifacts.\n- Alternative libraries: PyPDF2 (simpler), PyMuPDF/fitz (faster), camelot (table-focused).\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"2e2e7935-58a1-4186-87c9-770a7bb3f151"}
{"parentUuid":"b8a60a63-2693-478a-8b26-444c904d1884","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/root","sessionId":"3aa9576d-6447-425b-bde5-2cc7b09b0a07","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","slug":"jiggly-crunching-zebra","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_018y42NSbcMHtz5sWt3PcxnM","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /root/environment/skills/tanimoto-topk-sorting.md"}]},"uuid":"567641f6-10ec-4941-bd17-f56e43bc1586","timestamp":"2026-02-07T06:15:23.426Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/root/environment/skills/tanimoto-topk-sorting.md","content":"# Top-K Sorting with Tanimoto Similarity\n\n## Overview\nWhen finding the top-K most similar molecules, proper sorting is essential. Results should be sorted by descending similarity, with alphabetical ordering for ties.\n\n## Sorting Strategy\n\n### Python Sorting with Multiple Keys\n```python\n# Sort by similarity (descending), then by name (ascending) for ties\nresults = [(\"Aspirin\", 0.85), (\"Ibuprofen\", 0.92), (\"Naproxen\", 0.85)]\n\n# Method 1: Using sorted() with a tuple key\nsorted_results = sorted(results, key=lambda x: (-x[1], x[0]))\n# Result: [(\"Ibuprofen\", 0.92), (\"Aspirin\", 0.85), (\"Naproxen\", 0.85)]\n\n# Method 2: Extract top-k after sorting\ntop_k = sorted_results[:k]\n```\n\n### Complete Top-K Pipeline\n```python\ndef topk_similar(target_name, pool_names, pool_smiles, k):\n    \"\"\"Find top-k most similar molecules.\"\"\"\n    # 1. Compute target fingerprint\n    target_fp = get_fingerprint(target_smiles)\n\n    # 2. Compute similarities\n    similarities = []\n    for name, smiles in zip(pool_names, pool_smiles):\n        fp = get_fingerprint(smiles)\n        sim = tanimoto_similarity(target_fp, fp)\n        similarities.append((name, sim))\n\n    # 3. Sort: descending similarity, then alphabetical for ties\n    similarities.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[1], x[0]))\n\n    # 4. Return top-k names\n    return [name for name, sim in similarities[:k]]\n```\n\n## Important Notes\n- Negating the similarity value (`-x[1]`) converts ascending sort to descending.\n- Python's sort is stable, so the secondary key (alphabetical) is respected for equal primary keys.\n- When the target molecule appears in the pool, it will have similarity 1.0 — decide whether to include/exclude it based on requirements.\n- Alphabetical sorting should be case-sensitive by default (standard Python string comparison).\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"b8a60a63-2693-478a-8b26-444c904d1884"}
